| [Control room](#control-room-run-the-stack-from-here)                  | Start, scale, rebuild, test, and stop the stack without a terminal              |
| [Preflight](#preflight-is-everything-ready)                            | Whether Docker, the containers, Prometheus, Alertmanager, and the app are ready |
| [1. The question KEV check answers](#1-the-question-kev-check-answers) | The app, and what counts as an error                                            |
| [2. One request, five signals](#2-one-request-five-signals)            | Why each signal has its type, shown with a single request                       |
| [3. Histogram x-ray](#3-histogram-xray)                                | How bucket counts become a p95, worked out step by step                         |
| [4. Many copies](#4-many-copies)                                       | Adding up metrics across containers, through restarts, and series growth        |
| [5. Across services](#5-across-services)                               | Following one request across containers, and what changes with many services    |
| [6. Alerting](#6-alerting)                                             | Burn-rate alerts on an SLO, with faults you can turn on                         |
| [7. Chronosphere: shaping the data](#7-chronosphere-shaping-the-data)  | Drop, recording, roll-up, and derived rules, and the roll-up rule itself        |
| [The requirements, checked live](#the-requirements-checked-live)       | Every requirement for the app, graded against the running stack                 |
| [Wrap up](#wrap-up)                                                    | Reset the faults, and stop the stack                                            |

### Reading the Elixir

The Elixir that shows up in the code cells:

| You'll see                                                             | It means                                                                                                                                                      |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `defmodule Tour.Core.Quantile do ... end`                              | A module, a named group of functions. `Core` in the name means pure: data in, data out, no Docker, HTTP, or clock. Every other module is the shell around it. |
| `def estimate(q, buckets)` and `defp`                                  | A public function, and a private helper.                                                                                                                      |
| `@doc "..."` above `@spec estimate(float(), [bucket()]) :: estimate()` | One line on what the function returns, then its types: inputs before `::`, output after.                                                                      |
| `:ok`, `:inf`                                                          | Atoms, named constants like an enum value.                                                                                                                    |
| `{:ok, value}` and `{:error, "why"}`                                   | Tuples. A function that can fail returns one of these two shapes instead of throwing.                                                                         |
| `%{"le" => "0.25"}` and `%{name: "app-1"}`                             | Maps (dictionaries). The second form has atom keys, read with `map.name`.                                                                                     |
| `{:ok, body} = fetch()`                                                | `=` is a match: it checks the shape on the left and pulls `body` out of it.                                                                                   |
| Two `def`s with the same name                                          | Elixir runs the first clause whose arguments match. That's how the code branches instead of `if`.                                                             |
| `with {:ok, a} <- step1(), {:ok, b} <- step2(a) do`                    | Run steps in order and stop at the first one that doesn't return `{:ok, ...}`.                                                                                |
| `Enum.map(list, fn x -> x * 2 end)` and `&(&1 * 2)`                    | Loop over a list. `&(&1 * 2)` is shorthand for the same anonymous function.                                                                                   |
| `"#{name} is up"` and `~r/regex/`                                      | String interpolation, and a regular expression.                                                                                                               |
| `Kino.Control.button("Spin")`                                          | Kino is Livebook's UI library. This makes a button.                                                                                                           |
| `Kino.listen(button, fn _click -> ... end)`                            | Run the function every time the button is pressed.                                                                                                            |
| `Kino.Frame.new()`                                                     | A box on the page that code can redraw. That's how charts refresh.                                                                                            |

The pipe, `|>`, passes a value into the next call: `x |> f() |> g(2)` is `g(f(x), 2)`.

## Toolbox: the notebook's own code

The notebook's own modules. `Tour.Core.*` modules are pure functions with tests. The rest call Docker, Prometheus, and the app. Run this section once before the others.

`Tour.Config` holds paths and URLs. Livebook opened from the Dock gets a minimal `PATH`, so this cell adds the folders where `docker` usually lives.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Config do
  @moduledoc """
  Where things live: the repo checkout, the three local URLs, and the secret in `.env`.
  """

  @extra_path [
    "/usr/local/bin",
    "/opt/homebrew/bin",
    "~/.docker/bin",
    "/Applications/Docker.app/Contents/Resources/bin"
  ]

  @doc "The kev-check checkout: `KEV_CHECK_DIR` when set, otherwise the folder above this notebook."
  @spec repo() :: Path.t()
  def repo, do: System.get_env("KEV_CHECK_DIR") || Path.expand("..", __DIR__)

  @doc "The app, through the nginx proxy that spreads requests across the containers."
  @spec app_url() :: String.t()
  def app_url, do: "http://localhost:8080"

  @doc "Prometheus's UI and API."
  @spec prometheus_url() :: String.t()
  def prometheus_url, do: "http://localhost:9090"

  @doc "Alertmanager's UI and API."
  @spec alertmanager_url() :: String.t()
  def alertmanager_url, do: "http://localhost:9093"

  @doc "True when `.env` sets the `SECRET_KEY_BASE` Compose needs."
  @spec secret?() :: boolean()
  def secret? do
    case File.read(Path.join(repo(), ".env")) do
      {:ok, text} -> Regex.match?(~r/^SECRET_KEY_BASE=\S+/m, text)
      {:error, _missing} -> false
    end
  end

  @doc "Add a random `SECRET_KEY_BASE` to `.env`, unless it already sets one."
  @spec create_env() :: {:ok, String.t()} | {:error, String.t()}
  def create_env do
    case secret?() do
      true -> {:error, ".env already sets SECRET_KEY_BASE, so I left it alone."}
      false -> write_secret()
    end
  end

  @doc "Add Docker's and Homebrew's folders to the end of `PATH`."
  @spec extend_path() :: :ok
  def extend_path do
    path =
      System.get_env("PATH", "")
      |> String.split(":", trim: true)
      |> Enum.concat(Enum.map(@extra_path, &Path.expand/1))
      |> Enum.uniq()
      |> Enum.join(":")

    System.put_env("PATH", path)
  end

  defp write_secret do
    key = 48 |> :crypto.strong_rand_bytes() |> Base.encode64()

    case File.write(Path.join(repo(), ".env"), "SECRET_KEY_BASE=#{key}\n", [:append]) do
      :ok -> {:ok, "Wrote a random SECRET_KEY_BASE to .env."}
      {:error, reason} -> {:error, "Couldn't write .env: #{:file.format_error(reason)}"}
    end
  end
end

Tour.Config.extend_path()
ExUnit.start(autorun: false)
Tour.Config.repo()
```

`Tour.Shell` runs a command in the repo. `run/2` returns the output when the command finishes. `stream/3` passes output along as it arrives, for slow commands like a build.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Shell do
  @moduledoc """
  Runs a command in the repo folder, either collecting its output or streaming it chunk by
  chunk for slow work like a Docker build.
  """

  @type result :: {:ok, String.t()} | {:error, String.t()}

  @doc "Run `command` with `args` and return its combined output, tagged by whether it exited 0."
  @spec run(String.t(), [String.t()]) :: result()
  def run(command, args) do
    with {:ok, path} <- find(command) do
      {output, status} = System.cmd(path, args, cd: Tour.Config.repo(), stderr_to_stdout: true)
      tag(status, output)
    end
  end

  @doc "Like `run/2`, but hands each chunk of output to `on_output` as soon as it arrives."
  @spec stream(String.t(), [String.t()], (String.t() -> term())) :: result()
  def stream(command, args, on_output) do
    case find(command) do
      {:ok, path} ->
        {:spawn_executable, path}
        |> Port.open([
          :binary,
          :exit_status,
          :stderr_to_stdout,
          args: args,
          cd: Tour.Config.repo()
        ])
        |> collect(on_output, [])

      {:error, message} = error ->
        on_output.(message)
        error
    end
  end

  defp collect(port, on_output, acc) do
    receive do
      {^port, {:data, chunk}} ->
        on_output.(chunk)
        collect(port, on_output, [acc, chunk])

      {^port, {:exit_status, status}} ->
        tag(status, IO.iodata_to_binary(acc))
    end
  end

  defp find(command) do
    cond do
      not File.dir?(Tour.Config.repo()) ->
        {:error, "There's no checkout at #{Tour.Config.repo()}. Set KEV_CHECK_DIR."}

      path = System.find_executable(command) ->
        {:ok, path}

      true ->
        {:error, "#{command} isn't on PATH. Is it installed?"}
    end
  end

  defp tag(0, output), do: {:ok, output}
  defp tag(_status, output), do: {:error, output}
end
```

Pure modules. `PromJSON` converts Prometheus API values, which arrive as strings and can be `"NaN"` or `"+Inf"`. `Containers` parses `docker ps`. `Faults` builds the Elixir snippets that change a container's fault settings, and only accepts numbers in range. `LogLine` parses the app's JSON log lines.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Core.PromJSON do
  @moduledoc """
  Turns Prometheus API results into plain maps with numbers. Prometheus sends every value
  as a string, and "NaN" and "+Inf" can't be Elixir floats, so they become atoms.
  """

  @type value :: float() | :nan | :inf | :neg_inf
  @type sample :: %{labels: %{String.t() => String.t()}, value: value()}
  @type series :: %{labels: %{String.t() => String.t()}, points: [{integer(), value()}]}

  @doc "The samples in an instant query's `data`."
  @spec vector(map()) :: [sample()]
  def vector(%{"resultType" => "vector", "result" => result}), do: Enum.map(result, &sample/1)

  def vector(%{"resultType" => "scalar", "result" => [_time, value]}),
    do: [%{labels: %{}, value: value(value)}]

  @doc "The series in a range query's `data`, each point a unix time and a value."
  @spec matrix(map()) :: [series()]
  def matrix(%{"resultType" => "matrix", "result" => result}), do: Enum.map(result, &series/1)

  @doc "One value the way Prometheus writes it, as a float or a named non-number."
  @spec value(String.t()) :: value()
  def value("NaN"), do: :nan
  def value("+Inf"), do: :inf
  def value("-Inf"), do: :neg_inf

  def value(text) do
    {number, ""} = Float.parse(text)
    number
  end

  defp sample(%{"metric" => labels, "value" => [_time, value]}),
    do: %{labels: labels, value: value(value)}

  defp series(%{"metric" => labels, "values" => values}),
    do: %{
      labels: labels,
      points: Enum.map(values, fn [time, value] -> {trunc(time), value(value)} end)
    }
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Core.Containers do
  @moduledoc """
  Turns `docker ps --format '{{json .}}'` output, one JSON object per line, into plain maps.
  """

  @type t :: %{
          name: String.t(),
          service: String.t(),
          number: non_neg_integer(),
          state: String.t(),
          status: String.t(),
          ports: %{pos_integer() => pos_integer()}
        }

  @doc "Parse every line that is a container, sorted by service and then by replica number."
  @spec parse(String.t()) :: [t()]
  def parse(output) do
    output
    |> String.split("\n", trim: true)
    |> Enum.flat_map(&container/1)
    |> Enum.sort_by(&{&1.service, &1.number})
  end

  @doc "The app containers that are running, in replica order."
  @spec running_apps([t()]) :: [t()]
  def running_apps(containers),
    do: Enum.filter(containers, &match?(%{service: "app", state: "running"}, &1))

  defp container(line) do
    case JSON.decode(line) do
      {:ok, %{"Names" => name, "State" => state, "Status" => status, "Labels" => labels} = row} ->
        [
          %{
            name: name,
            service: label(labels, "com.docker.compose.service"),
            number: labels |> label("com.docker.compose.container-number") |> to_integer(),
            state: state,
            status: status,
            ports: ports(Map.get(row, "Ports", ""))
          }
        ]

      _not_a_container ->
        []
    end
  end

  defp label(labels, key) do
    case Regex.run(~r/(?:^|,)#{Regex.escape(key)}=([^,]*)/, labels, capture: :all_but_first) do
      [value] -> value
      nil -> ""
    end
  end

  # "127.0.0.1:59918->4000/tcp" means container port 4000 is host port 59918.
  defp ports(text) do
    for [host, container] <- Regex.scan(~r/:(\d+)->(\d+)\/tcp/, text, capture: :all_but_first),
        into: %{},
        do: {String.to_integer(container), String.to_integer(host)}
  end

  defp to_integer(""), do: 0
  defp to_integer(text), do: String.to_integer(text)
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Core.Faults do
  @moduledoc """
  Writes the one-line Elixir snippets that read or change an app container's fault knobs,
  and reads their JSON answers. Only numbers that pass the guards reach a snippet.
  """

  @type settings :: %{latency_ms: non_neg_integer(), error_rate: float()}
  @type status :: %{current: settings(), boot: settings() | nil}

  @doc "A snippet that prints the knobs in effect and the boot values from config."
  @spec status_code() :: String.t()
  def status_code,
    do:
      "IO.puts(JSON.encode!(%{current: KevCheck.Faults.current(), " <>
        "boot: Map.new(Application.get_env(:kev_check, KevCheck.Faults))}))"

  @doc "A snippet that sets both knobs, or why the values were refused."
  @spec put_code(term(), term()) :: {:ok, String.t()} | {:error, String.t()}
  def put_code(latency_ms, error_rate)
      when is_integer(latency_ms) and latency_ms in 0..5_000 and is_number(error_rate) and
             error_rate >= 0 and error_rate <= 1 do
    {:ok,
     "IO.puts(JSON.encode!(%{current: KevCheck.Faults.put(" <>
       "latency_ms: #{latency_ms}, error_rate: #{error_rate * 1.0})}))"}
  end

  def put_code(latency_ms, error_rate),
    do:
      {:error,
       "Latency has to be 0 to 5000 ms and the error rate 0 to 1, " <>
         "got #{inspect(latency_ms)} and #{inspect(error_rate)}."}

  @doc "A snippet that puts the knobs back to their boot values."
  @spec reset_code() :: String.t()
  def reset_code, do: "IO.puts(JSON.encode!(%{current: KevCheck.Faults.reset()}))"

  @doc "Read the knobs from a snippet's output, whose last line is the JSON."
  @spec parse(String.t()) :: {:ok, status()} | {:error, String.t()}
  def parse(output) do
    with [line | _earlier] <- output |> String.split("\n", trim: true) |> Enum.reverse(),
         {:ok, %{"current" => current} = answer} <- JSON.decode(line) do
      {:ok, %{current: settings(current), boot: settings(answer["boot"])}}
    else
      _unreadable -> {:error, String.trim(output)}
    end
  end

  @doc "The knobs in words, like `up to 400 ms, 20% errors`."
  @spec describe(settings()) :: String.t()
  def describe(%{latency_ms: ms, error_rate: rate}),
    do: "up to #{ms} ms, #{round(rate * 100)}% errors"

  defp settings(%{"latency_ms" => ms, "error_rate" => rate}),
    do: %{latency_ms: ms, error_rate: rate * 1.0}

  defp settings(nil), do: nil
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Core.LogLine do
  @moduledoc """
  Reads the app's JSON log lines into flat rows. Anything that isn't one of the app's JSON
  lines is skipped.
  """

  @type row :: %{
          time: String.t(),
          pod: String.t(),
          level: String.t(),
          message: String.t(),
          outcome: String.t() | nil,
          reason: String.t() | nil,
          cve_id: String.t() | nil,
          duration_ms: number() | nil,
          request_id: String.t() | nil
        }

  @doc "Every JSON log line in `output`, oldest first, tagged with the container it came from."
  @spec rows(String.t(), String.t()) :: [row()]
  def rows(output, pod) do
    output
    |> String.split("\n", trim: true)
    |> Enum.flat_map(&row(&1, pod))
  end

  @doc "The raw line that carries `request_id`, exactly as the app wrote it."
  @spec find(String.t(), String.t() | nil) :: {:ok, String.t()} | :error
  def find(_output, nil), do: :error

  def find(output, request_id) do
    needle = ~s("request_id":"#{request_id}")

    case output |> String.split("\n") |> Enum.find(&String.contains?(&1, needle)) do
      nil -> :error
      line -> {:ok, line}
    end
  end

  defp row(line, pod) do
    case JSON.decode(line) do
      {:ok, %{"message" => message, "time" => time, "severity" => level} = entry} ->
        meta = Map.get(entry, "metadata", %{})

        [
          %{
            time: time,
            pod: pod,
            level: level,
            message: message,
            outcome: meta["outcome"],
            reason: meta["reason"],
            cve_id: meta["cve_id"],
            duration_ms: meta["duration_ms"],
            request_id: meta["request_id"]
          }
        ]

      _not_ours ->
        []
    end
  end
end
```

Fault settings end up as code inside a container, so this checks that only numbers in range get through.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.FaultsTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  alias Tour.Core.Faults

  test "fault snippets only take numbers in range" do
    assert {:ok, code} = Faults.put_code(400, 0.2)
    assert code =~ "latency_ms: 400, error_rate: 0.2"

    for {ms, rate} <- [{-1, 0}, {5_001, 0}, {0, 1.5}, {"400", 0}, {400, "0.2); System.halt("}] do
      assert {:error, _why} = Faults.put_code(ms, rate)
    end
  end
end

ExUnit.run()
```

`Tour.Docker` wraps the Docker CLI. It only acts on running app containers of this Compose project.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Docker do
  @moduledoc """
  The Docker CLI, aimed only at this Compose project: list its containers, run Elixir in an
  app container, read logs, and pause the load generator. Anything that touches a container
  first checks it's one of this project's running app containers.
  """

  alias Tour.Core.Containers
  alias Tour.Core.Faults
  alias Tour.Core.LogLine

  @project "label=com.docker.compose.project=kev-check"

  @doc "Every container in the kev-check project, running or not."
  @spec stack() :: {:ok, [Containers.t()]} | {:error, String.t()}
  def stack do
    args = ["ps", "--all", "--filter", @project, "--format", "{{json .}}"]
    with {:ok, output} <- Tour.Shell.run("docker", args), do: {:ok, Containers.parse(output)}
  end

  @doc "The app containers running right now, or none when Docker can't say."
  @spec apps() :: [Containers.t()]
  def apps do
    case stack() do
      {:ok, containers} -> Containers.running_apps(containers)
      {:error, _reason} -> []
    end
  end

  @doc "Evaluate one line of Elixir in a running app container, through the release's `rpc` command."
  @spec rpc(String.t(), String.t()) :: Tour.Shell.result()
  def rpc(name, code) do
    with :ok <- guard(name),
         do: Tour.Shell.run("docker", ["exec", name, "bin/kev_check", "rpc", code])
  end

  @doc "A container's fault knobs, now and at boot."
  @spec faults(String.t()) :: {:ok, Faults.status()} | {:error, String.t()}
  def faults(name) do
    with {:ok, output} <- rpc(name, Faults.status_code()), do: Faults.parse(output)
  end

  @doc "Set a container's fault knobs."
  @spec put_faults(String.t(), term(), term()) :: {:ok, Faults.status()} | {:error, String.t()}
  def put_faults(name, latency_ms, error_rate) do
    with {:ok, code} <- Faults.put_code(latency_ms, error_rate),
         {:ok, output} <- rpc(name, code),
         do: Faults.parse(output)
  end

  @doc "Put a container's fault knobs back to their boot values."
  @spec reset_faults(String.t()) :: {:ok, Faults.status()} | {:error, String.t()}
  def reset_faults(name) do
    with {:ok, output} <- rpc(name, Faults.reset_code()), do: Faults.parse(output)
  end

  @doc "Reset every app container's knobs and unpause the load generator, one row per action."
  @spec reset_all() :: [{String.t(), {:ok, term()} | {:error, String.t()}}]
  def reset_all do
    faults = Enum.map(apps(), &{&1.name, reset_faults(&1.name)})
    faults ++ [{"load generator", running(loadgen(:unpause))}]
  end

  @doc "Crash a container's BEAM with exit code 1. Docker restarts it under the same name."
  @spec crash(String.t()) :: :ok | {:error, String.t()}
  def crash(name) do
    with :ok <- guard(name) do
      # The node dies mid-call, so rpc always reports a failure here.
      _ = Tour.Shell.run("docker", ["exec", name, "bin/kev_check", "rpc", "System.halt(1)"])
      :ok
    end
  end

  @doc "The last `lines` log lines of a running app container."
  @spec logs(String.t(), pos_integer()) :: Tour.Shell.result()
  def logs(name, lines) do
    with :ok <- guard(name),
         do: Tour.Shell.run("docker", ["logs", "--tail", Integer.to_string(lines), name])
  end

  @doc "The container and log line carrying `request_id`. Logger writes just after the response, so this retries briefly."
  @spec find_log(String.t() | nil) :: {:ok, String.t(), String.t()} | :error
  def find_log(nil), do: :error

  def find_log(request_id) do
    Enum.find_value(1..5, :error, fn _try ->
      case Enum.find_value(apps(), &log_match(&1.name, request_id)) do
        nil ->
          Process.sleep(200)
          nil

        found ->
          found
      end
    end)
  end

  @doc "Freeze or thaw the load generator, so a measurement sees only the notebook's request."
  @spec loadgen(:pause | :unpause) :: Tour.Shell.result()
  def loadgen(action) when action in [:pause, :unpause],
    do: Tour.Shell.run("docker", [Atom.to_string(action), "kev-check-loadgen-1"])

  defp log_match(name, request_id) do
    with {:ok, output} <- logs(name, 300),
         {:ok, line} <- LogLine.find(output, request_id) do
      {:ok, name, line}
    else
      _not_here -> nil
    end
  end

  # Unpausing a load generator that wasn't paused is the outcome we wanted anyway.
  defp running({:error, output}) do
    if output =~ "is not paused", do: {:ok, "running"}, else: {:error, output}
  end

  defp running(result), do: result

  defp guard(name) do
    case Enum.find(apps(), &(&1.name == name)) do
      nil -> {:error, "#{name} isn't a running kev-check app container, so I won't touch it."}
      _container -> :ok
    end
  end
end
```

`Tour.Prom` and `Tour.Alertmanager` call the two HTTP APIs. Errors come back as `{:error, message}`, so cells still render when the stack is down.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Prom do
  @moduledoc """
  Prometheus's HTTP API: instant and range queries, alert rules, scrape targets, and how much
  history it holds. Failures come back as sentences, never exceptions.
  """

  alias Tour.Core.PromJSON

  @doc "Run an instant query now, or at the unix `:time` in `opts`."
  @spec query(String.t(), keyword()) :: {:ok, [PromJSON.sample()]} | {:error, String.t()}
  def query(expr, opts \\ []) do
    with {:ok, data} <- get("/api/v1/query", [query: expr] ++ Keyword.take(opts, [:time])),
         do: {:ok, PromJSON.vector(data)}
  end

  @doc "Run a range query over the last `minutes`, one point every `step` seconds."
  @spec range(String.t(), pos_integer(), pos_integer()) ::
          {:ok, [PromJSON.series()]} | {:error, String.t()}
  def range(expr, minutes \\ 15, step \\ 15) do
    now = System.os_time(:second)
    params = [query: expr, start: now - minutes * 60, end: now, step: step]
    with {:ok, data} <- get("/api/v1/query_range", params), do: {:ok, PromJSON.matrix(data)}
  end

  @doc "Every alerting rule, with its state and any active alerts."
  @spec alert_rules() :: {:ok, [map()]} | {:error, String.t()}
  def alert_rules do
    with {:ok, %{"groups" => groups}} <- get("/api/v1/rules", type: "alert"),
         do: {:ok, Enum.flat_map(groups, & &1["rules"])}
  end

  @doc "The targets Prometheus is scraping."
  @spec targets() :: {:ok, [map()]} | {:error, String.t()}
  def targets do
    with {:ok, %{"activeTargets" => targets}} <- get("/api/v1/targets", state: "active"),
         do: {:ok, targets}
  end

  @doc "Minutes between the oldest and newest sample in Prometheus's in-memory block."
  @spec history_minutes() :: {:ok, non_neg_integer()} | {:error, String.t()}
  def history_minutes do
    with {:ok, %{"headStats" => %{"minTime" => first, "maxTime" => last}}} <-
           get("/api/v1/status/tsdb", []),
         do: {:ok, max(div(last - first, 60_000), 0)}
  end

  @doc "True when Prometheus says it's ready."
  @spec ready?() :: boolean()
  def ready?,
    do:
      match?(
        {:ok, %{status: 200}},
        Req.get(Tour.Config.prometheus_url() <> "/-/ready", retry: false)
      )

  defp get(path, params) do
    url = Tour.Config.prometheus_url() <> path

    case Req.get(url, params: params, retry: false, receive_timeout: 10_000) do
      {:ok, %Req.Response{body: %{"status" => "success", "data" => data}}} ->
        {:ok, data}

      {:ok, %Req.Response{body: %{"error" => error}}} ->
        {:error, "Prometheus refused the query: #{error}"}

      {:ok, %Req.Response{status: status}} ->
        {:error, "Prometheus answered HTTP #{status}."}

      {:error, exception} ->
        {:error, "Prometheus isn't answering at #{url}: #{Exception.message(exception)}"}
    end
  end
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Alertmanager do
  @moduledoc """
  Alertmanager's API: whether it's up, and the alerts it holds with the receiver each one
  was routed to.
  """

  @doc "True when Alertmanager says it's ready."
  @spec ready?() :: boolean()
  def ready?,
    do:
      match?(
        {:ok, %{status: 200}},
        Req.get(Tour.Config.alertmanager_url() <> "/-/ready", retry: false)
      )

  @doc "The alerts Alertmanager holds, with their receivers and anything muting them."
  @spec alerts() :: {:ok, [map()]} | {:error, String.t()}
  def alerts do
    case Req.get(Tour.Config.alertmanager_url() <> "/api/v2/alerts", retry: false) do
      {:ok, %Req.Response{status: 200, body: alerts}} when is_list(alerts) ->
        {:ok, alerts}

      {:ok, %Req.Response{status: status}} ->
        {:error, "Alertmanager answered HTTP #{status}."}

      {:error, exception} ->
        {:error, "Alertmanager isn't answering: #{Exception.message(exception)}"}
    end
  end
end
```

`Tour.App` calls the app through the proxy on port 8080, or directly on one container's published port.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.App do
  @moduledoc """
  HTTP calls to KEV check: a lookup through the proxy or straight at one container, and one
  container's `/metrics` page.
  """

  @type reply :: %{status: pos_integer(), body: term(), request_id: String.t() | nil, ms: float()}

  @doc "Look up `id` through the proxy, or at `base` when given."
  @spec lookup(String.t(), String.t()) :: {:ok, reply()} | {:error, String.t()}
  def lookup(id, base \\ Tour.Config.app_url()) do
    {micros, result} =
      :timer.tc(fn ->
        Req.get(base <> "/process", params: [id: id], retry: false, receive_timeout: 10_000)
      end)

    case result do
      {:ok, %Req.Response{status: status, body: body} = response} ->
        request_id = response |> Req.Response.get_header("x-request-id") |> List.first()
        {:ok, %{status: status, body: body, request_id: request_id, ms: micros / 1000}}

      {:error, exception} ->
        {:error, "The app isn't answering at #{base}: #{Exception.message(exception)}"}
    end
  end

  @doc "The base URL of one container's own API port, skipping the proxy."
  @spec direct(Tour.Core.Containers.t()) :: String.t()
  def direct(%{ports: %{4000 => port}}), do: "http://localhost:#{port}"

  @doc "The Prometheus text one container serves on its metrics port."
  @spec metrics(pos_integer() | nil) :: {:ok, String.t()} | {:error, String.t()}
  def metrics(nil), do: {:error, "That container has no published metrics port."}

  def metrics(port) do
    case Req.get("http://localhost:#{port}/metrics", retry: false, receive_timeout: 5_000) do
      {:ok, %Req.Response{status: 200, body: body}} -> {:ok, body}
      {:ok, %Req.Response{status: status}} -> {:error, "/metrics answered HTTP #{status}."}
      {:error, exception} -> {:error, "/metrics isn't answering: #{Exception.message(exception)}"}
    end
  end
end
```

Display helpers. `Tour.UI` has status marks, Markdown tables, and Prometheus links. `Tour.Charts` builds chart specs. `Tour.Live` refreshes frames and charts every 5 seconds.

````elixir
defmodule Tour.UI do
  @moduledoc """
  Presentation helpers every section shares: status marks, Markdown tables, links into the
  Prometheus UI, and drawing into frames so a failure shows up as a sentence.
  """

  @doc "A frame that shows `text` until something replaces it."
  @spec frame(String.t()) :: Kino.Frame.t()
  def frame(text \\ "") do
    frame = Kino.Frame.new(placeholder: false)
    Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Markdown.new(text))
    frame
  end

  @doc "Draw whatever `fun` returns into `frame`. An exception becomes a red note instead of a dead button."
  @spec draw(Kino.Frame.t(), (-> term())) :: :ok
  def draw(frame, fun) do
    Kino.Frame.render(frame, fun.())
  rescue
    error -> Kino.Frame.render(frame, note(:error, Exception.message(error)))
  end

  @doc "Run a command and stream its output into `frame` like a terminal, ending with a status line."
  @spec run_in(Kino.Frame.t(), String.t(), [String.t()]) :: Tour.Shell.result()
  def run_in(frame, command, args) do
    Kino.Frame.render(frame, Kino.Markdown.new("`$ #{Enum.join([command | args], " ")}`"))
    append = &Kino.Frame.append(frame, Kino.Text.new(&1, terminal: true, chunk: true))
    result = Tour.Shell.stream(command, args, append)
    Kino.Frame.append(frame, finished(result))
    result
  end

  @doc "Reset every app container and the load generator, and show where each one landed."
  @spec reset_everything() :: Kino.Markdown.t()
  def reset_everything do
    rows = for {name, result} <- Tour.Docker.reset_all(), do: reset_row(name, result)
    table(["", "Reset", "Now"], rows)
  end

  @doc "Several outputs, one under the other."
  @spec stack([term()]) :: Kino.Layout.t()
  def stack(items), do: Kino.Layout.grid(items, columns: 1)

  @doc "A one-line note with a status mark."
  @spec note(atom(), String.t()) :: Kino.Markdown.t()
  def note(level, text), do: Kino.Markdown.new("#{mark(level)} #{text}")

  @doc "The mark for a status or an alert state."
  @spec mark(atom() | String.t()) :: String.t()
  def mark(:ok), do: "✅"
  def mark(:warn), do: "⚠️"
  def mark(:error), do: "❌"
  def mark(:info), do: "👉"
  def mark("firing"), do: "🔥"
  def mark("pending"), do: "🟡"
  def mark(_inactive), do: "⚪"

  @doc "A Markdown table from a header and rows of cells."
  @spec table([String.t()], [[term()]]) :: Kino.Markdown.t()
  def table(header, rows) do
    divider = Enum.map(header, fn _column -> "---" end)
    Kino.Markdown.new(Enum.map_join([header, divider | rows], "\n", &row/1))
  end

  @doc "A Markdown link that opens `expr` as a 15 minute graph in the Prometheus UI."
  @spec prom_link(String.t(), String.t()) :: String.t()
  def prom_link(expr, label \\ "Open in Prometheus") do
    query = URI.encode_query([{"g0.expr", expr}, {"g0.tab", "graph"}, {"g0.range_input", "15m"}])
    "[#{label} ↗](#{Tour.Config.prometheus_url()}/query?#{query})"
  end

  @doc "A PromQL query as a code block, with its Prometheus link under it."
  @spec promql(String.t()) :: Kino.Markdown.t()
  def promql(expr), do: Kino.Markdown.new("```promql\n#{expr}\n```\n\n#{prom_link(expr)}")

  @doc "A JSON log line as a pretty-printed code block, keys in the order the app wrote them."
  @spec json_block(String.t()) :: String.t()
  def json_block(line) do
    pretty =
      case Jason.decode(line, objects: :ordered_objects) do
        {:ok, decoded} -> Jason.encode!(decoded, pretty: true)
        {:error, _not_json} -> line
      end

    "```json\n#{pretty}\n```"
  end

  @doc "Seconds as milliseconds, or what a missing number means."
  @spec ms(term()) :: String.t()
  def ms(seconds) when is_number(seconds) and seconds < 0.001,
    do: "#{Float.round(seconds * 1000.0, 3)} ms"

  def ms(seconds) when is_number(seconds), do: "#{Float.round(seconds * 1000.0, 1)} ms"
  def ms(_other), do: "no data"

  @doc "An integer with thousands separators, like 1,152,000."
  @spec count(integer()) :: String.t()
  def count(n) when is_integer(n) do
    n
    |> Integer.to_string()
    |> String.reverse()
    |> String.replace(~r/(\d{3})(?=\d)/, "\\1,")
    |> String.reverse()
  end

  @doc "A ratio as a percent, or what a missing number means."
  @spec percent(term()) :: String.t()
  def percent(ratio) when is_number(ratio), do: "#{Float.round(ratio * 100.0, 2)}%"
  def percent(:nan), do: "no traffic"
  def percent(_other), do: "no data"

  defp row(cells), do: "| " <> Enum.map_join(cells, " | ", &cell/1) <> " |"

  defp cell(nil), do: ""

  defp cell(value),
    do: value |> to_string() |> String.replace("|", "\\|") |> String.replace("\n", " ")

  defp reset_row(name, {:ok, %{current: settings}}),
    do: [mark(:ok), name, Tour.Core.Faults.describe(settings)]

  defp reset_row(name, {:ok, _output}), do: [mark(:ok), name, "running"]

  defp reset_row(name, {:error, reason}),
    do: [mark(:warn), name, reason |> String.split("\n", trim: true) |> List.first("")]

  defp finished({:ok, _output}), do: note(:ok, "Done.")
  defp finished({:error, _output}), do: note(:error, "That failed. The output above says why.")
end
````

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Charts do
  @moduledoc """
  VegaLite specs and rows for the live charts. Rows in, chart out, nothing fetched here.
  """

  alias VegaLite, as: Vl

  @doc "Flatten range-query series into chart rows named by `name`, leaving out non-numbers."
  @spec rows([Tour.Core.PromJSON.series()], (map() -> String.t())) :: [map()]
  def rows(series, name), do: Enum.flat_map(series, &points(&1, name.(&1.labels)))

  @doc "Rows that draw each `{label, value}` as a flat dashed line across the time `rows` cover."
  @spec flat([map()], [{String.t(), number()}]) :: [map()]
  def flat([], _thresholds), do: []

  def flat(rows, thresholds) do
    {first, last} = rows |> Enum.map(& &1.time) |> Enum.min_max()

    for {label, value} <- thresholds,
        time <- [first, last],
        do: %{time: time, value: value, series: label, kind: "threshold"}
  end

  @doc "An empty line chart, one line per `series`, ready for rows to be pushed in."
  @spec lines(String.t(), keyword()) :: VegaLite.t()
  def lines(title, opts \\ []) do
    Vl.new(width: Keyword.get(opts, :width, 330), height: 200, title: title)
    |> Vl.mark(:line, interpolate: "monotone")
    |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "time", type: :temporal, title: nil)
    |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "value", type: :quantitative, title: Keyword.get(opts, :unit, ""))
    |> Vl.encode_field(:color, "series",
      type: :nominal,
      title: nil,
      legend: [orient: "bottom", columns: 3]
    )
    |> Vl.encode_field(:stroke_dash, "kind", type: :nominal, legend: nil)
  end

  defp points(%{points: points}, series) do
    for {time, value} <- points,
        is_number(value),
        do: %{time: time * 1000, value: value, series: series, kind: "data"}
  end
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Live do
  @moduledoc """
  Live panels: frames and charts that refresh every 5 seconds while the notebook is open.
  """

  @every_ms 5_000

  @doc "A frame that draws `draw.()` now and again every 5 seconds."
  @spec panel((-> term())) :: Kino.Frame.t()
  def panel(draw) do
    frame = Kino.Frame.new(placeholder: false)
    Tour.UI.draw(frame, draw)
    Kino.listen(@every_ms, fn _tick -> Tour.UI.draw(frame, draw) end)
    frame
  end

  @doc """
  A live line chart of `{name, expr}` range queries over the last 15 minutes. A name like
  `"{pod}"` takes each series' `pod` label, and each `{label, value}` in `:thresholds`
  draws as a dashed line. The chart is empty while Prometheus is down.
  """
  @spec chart(String.t(), [{String.t(), String.t()}], keyword()) :: Kino.VegaLite.t()
  def chart(title, queries, opts \\ []) do
    chart = Kino.VegaLite.new(Tour.Charts.lines(title, opts))
    thresholds = Keyword.get(opts, :thresholds, [])
    refresh(chart, queries, thresholds)
    Kino.listen(@every_ms, fn _tick -> refresh(chart, queries, thresholds) end)
    chart
  end

  # A push whose window equals its own length replaces all the data in one step, so the chart
  # never blanks between updates. Live charts stay out of frames: inside one, Livebook can
  # grow them without limit.
  defp refresh(chart, queries, thresholds) do
    rows =
      case fetch(queries) do
        {:ok, rows} -> rows ++ Tour.Charts.flat(rows, thresholds)
        {:error, _down} -> []
      end

    Kino.VegaLite.push_many(chart, rows, window: length(rows))
  end

  defp fetch(queries) do
    Enum.reduce_while(queries, {:ok, []}, fn {name, expr}, {:ok, rows} ->
      case Tour.Prom.range(expr) do
        {:ok, series} -> {:cont, {:ok, rows ++ Tour.Charts.rows(series, &label(name, &1))}}
        {:error, reason} -> {:halt, {:error, reason}}
      end
    end)
  end

  # "{pod}" becomes "app-1": every container name starts with the project's.
  defp label(name, labels) do
    Regex.replace(~r/\{(\w+)\}/, name, fn _whole, key ->
      labels |> Map.get(key, "") |> String.replace_prefix("kev-check-", "")
    end)
  end
end
```

## Control room: run the stack from here

Each button runs a `make` target from the repo. The container table refreshes every 5 seconds, and command output appears below it.

| Button        | Runs            | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                  |
| ------------- | --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| ▶ Start     | `make up`       | Builds the image and starts two app containers, the nginx proxy on :8080, Prometheus on :9090, Alertmanager on :9093, and a load generator. About 20 seconds once the base images are pulled. |
| ■ Stop      | `make down`     | Stops and removes the containers, keeping Prometheus's history.                                                                                                                               |
| Reset faults  |                 | Puts every container's fault knobs back to their boot values and unpauses the load generator.                                                                                                 |
| Rebuild app   | `make redeploy` | Rebuilds the image and recreates the app containers, for a code change.                                                                                                                       |
| Reload rules  | `make reload`   | Checks the config and runs the rule tests, then reloads Prometheus and Alertmanager.                                                                                                          |
| Run tests     | `make check`    | Runs the formatter, the compiler, Credo, the tests, and Dialyzer inside Docker. A few minutes the first time.                                                                                 |
| Create .env   |                 | Adds a random `SECRET_KEY_BASE` to `.env` if it doesn't set one. Compose reads `.env` on every command.                                                                                       |
| Scale         | `make scale`    | Changes how many app containers run. Prometheus finds new ones through the Docker socket within seconds.                                                                                      |
| Stop and wipe | `make clean`    | Like Stop, and deletes Prometheus's history, which empties the 1 hour alert windows. Requires the checkbox.                                                                                   |

```elixir
buttons = [
  start: Kino.Control.button("▶ Start"),
  down: Kino.Control.button("■ Stop"),
  reset: Kino.Control.button("Reset faults"),
  redeploy: Kino.Control.button("Rebuild app"),
  reload: Kino.Control.button("Reload rules"),
  check: Kino.Control.button("Run tests"),
  env: Kino.Control.button("Create .env")
]

scale =
  Kino.Control.form(
    [
      n:
        Kino.Input.select("App containers", Enum.map(1..5, &{&1, Integer.to_string(&1)}),
          default: 2
        )
    ],
    submit: "Scale"
  )

wipe =
  Kino.Control.form([sure: Kino.Input.checkbox("Also delete Prometheus's history")],
    submit: "Stop and wipe"
  )

output = Tour.UI.frame("The last command's output shows up here.")

secret = fn ->
  case Tour.Config.secret?() do
    true -> Tour.UI.note(:ok, "`SECRET_KEY_BASE` is set in `.env`.")
    false -> Tour.UI.note(:error, "`.env` has no `SECRET_KEY_BASE` yet. Press Create .env.")
  end
end

board = fn ->
  case Tour.Docker.stack() do
    {:ok, []} ->
      Tour.UI.stack([secret.(), Tour.UI.note(:info, "Nothing is running. Press Start.")])

    {:ok, containers} ->
      rows =
        Enum.map(containers, &[&1.service, &1.name, &1.status, &1.ports[4000], &1.ports[9568]])

      Tour.UI.stack([
        secret.(),
        Tour.UI.table(["Service", "Container", "Status", "API", "Metrics"], rows)
      ])

    {:error, reason} ->
      Tour.UI.note(:error, reason)
  end
end

env_note = fn
  {:ok, message} -> Tour.UI.note(:ok, message)
  {:error, message} -> Tour.UI.note(:warn, message)
end

Kino.listen(Kino.Control.tagged_stream([scale: scale, wipe: wipe] ++ buttons), fn
  {:start, _click} -> Tour.UI.run_in(output, "make", ["up"])
  {:down, _click} -> Tour.UI.run_in(output, "make", ["down"])
  {:reset, _click} -> Tour.UI.draw(output, &Tour.UI.reset_everything/0)
  {:redeploy, _click} -> Tour.UI.run_in(output, "make", ["redeploy"])
  {:reload, _click} -> Tour.UI.run_in(output, "make", ["reload"])
  {:check, _click} -> Tour.UI.run_in(output, "make", ["check"])
  {:env, _click} -> Tour.UI.draw(output, fn -> env_note.(Tour.Config.create_env()) end)
  {:scale, %{data: %{n: n}}} -> Tour.UI.run_in(output, "make", ["scale", "N=#{n}"])
  {:wipe, %{data: %{sure: true}}} -> Tour.UI.run_in(output, "make", ["clean"])
  {:wipe, _unsure} -> Kino.Frame.render(output, Tour.UI.note(:warn, "Tick the box first."))
end)

links =
  Kino.Markdown.new(
    "[App ↗](http://localhost:8080/process?id=CVE-2021-44228) · " <>
      "[Prometheus ↗](http://localhost:9090/targets) · [Alertmanager ↗](http://localhost:9093)"
  )

Tour.UI.stack([
  links,
  Kino.Layout.grid(Keyword.values(buttons), columns: 4),
  Kino.Layout.grid([scale, wipe], columns: 2),
  Tour.Live.panel(board),
  output
])
```

## Preflight: is everything ready?

Checks Docker, the secret, the containers, Prometheus, Alertmanager, the app, how much history Prometheus has, and the fault settings. Run it after Start.

`Tour.Core.Preflight` turns the gathered facts into checks. `Tour.Preflight` gathers them.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Core.Preflight do
  @moduledoc """
  Turns what the preflight gathered into checks marked ok, warn, or error, each with a
  sentence saying what's true or what to do about it.
  """

  @type level :: :ok | :warn | :error
  @type check :: %{name: String.t(), level: level(), detail: String.t()}

  @services ~w(app proxy prometheus alertmanager loadgen)

  @doc "Every check, in the order a person would fix them."
  @spec checks(map()) :: [check()]
  def checks(facts) do
    [
      docker(facts.docker),
      checkout(facts.repo, facts.repo_path),
      secret(facts.secret),
      containers(facts.stack),
      prometheus(facts.prometheus),
      targets(facts.targets),
      rules(facts.rules),
      alertmanager(facts.alertmanager),
      app(facts.lookup),
      history(facts.history),
      faults(facts.faults)
    ]
  end

  defp docker({:ok, version}),
    do: check("Docker", :ok, "Engine #{String.trim(version)} is running.")

  defp docker({:error, reason}),
    do: check("Docker", :error, "#{first_line(reason)} Start Docker Desktop.")

  defp checkout(true, path), do: check("Checkout", :ok, "Found `compose.yaml` in `#{path}`.")

  defp checkout(false, path),
    do:
      check(
        "Checkout",
        :error,
        "No `compose.yaml` in `#{path}`. Set `KEV_CHECK_DIR` and rerun the Toolbox."
      )

  defp secret(true), do: check("Secret", :ok, "`SECRET_KEY_BASE` is set in `.env`.")

  defp secret(false),
    do:
      check(
        "Secret",
        :error,
        "`.env` has no `SECRET_KEY_BASE`. Press Create .env in the Control room."
      )

  defp containers({:ok, containers}) do
    running = for %{state: "running", service: service} <- containers, do: service
    paused = for %{state: "paused", service: service} <- containers, do: service
    apps = Enum.count(running, &(&1 == "app"))

    case {@services -- (running ++ paused), paused} do
      {[], []} ->
        check(
          "Containers",
          :ok,
          "#{apps} app containers, the proxy, Prometheus, Alertmanager, and the load generator."
        )

      {[], paused} ->
        check(
          "Containers",
          :warn,
          "Paused: #{Enum.join(paused, ", ")}. Reset in the Control room unpauses it."
        )

      {missing, _paused} ->
        check(
          "Containers",
          :error,
          "Not running: #{Enum.join(missing, ", ")}. Press Start in the Control room."
        )
    end
  end

  defp containers({:error, reason}), do: check("Containers", :error, first_line(reason))

  defp prometheus(true), do: check("Prometheus", :ok, "Ready on localhost:9090.")
  defp prometheus(false), do: check("Prometheus", :error, "Not answering on localhost:9090.")

  defp targets({:ok, targets}) do
    apps = Enum.filter(targets, &(get_in(&1, ["labels", "job"]) == "kev-check"))

    case {Enum.count(apps, &(&1["health"] == "up")), length(apps)} do
      {0, 0} ->
        check("Scrape targets", :error, "Prometheus hasn't found an app container yet.")

      {all, all} ->
        check("Scrape targets", :ok, "#{all} of #{all} app containers scraped and up.")

      {up, total} ->
        check(
          "Scrape targets",
          :warn,
          "#{up} of #{total} up. A restarted container is back after one 15 second scrape."
        )
    end
  end

  defp targets({:error, reason}), do: check("Scrape targets", :error, reason)

  defp rules({:ok, rules}), do: check("Alert rules", :ok, "#{length(rules)} alert rules loaded.")
  defp rules({:error, reason}), do: check("Alert rules", :error, reason)

  defp alertmanager(true), do: check("Alertmanager", :ok, "Ready on localhost:9093.")
  defp alertmanager(false), do: check("Alertmanager", :error, "Not answering on localhost:9093.")

  defp app({:ok, %{status: 200, body: %{"catalog_version" => version}}}),
    do: check("App", :ok, "Answers through the proxy on :8080, serving catalog #{version}.")

  defp app({:ok, %{status: status}}),
    do: check("App", :error, "The proxy answered HTTP #{status}.")

  defp app({:error, reason}), do: check("App", :error, reason)

  defp history({:ok, minutes}) when minutes >= 60,
    do: check("History", :ok, "#{minutes} minutes of data, enough for the 1 hour alert windows.")

  defp history({:ok, minutes}) when minutes >= 15,
    do:
      check(
        "History",
        :warn,
        "#{minutes} minutes so far. Charts look full; the 1 hour alert windows fill at 60."
      )

  defp history({:ok, minutes}),
    do:
      check(
        "History",
        :warn,
        "Only #{minutes} minutes so far. Charts look full after 15, alert windows after 60."
      )

  defp history({:error, reason}), do: check("History", :error, reason)

  defp faults([]), do: check("Faults", :warn, "No app container to ask.")

  defp faults(results) do
    case Enum.reject(results, &boot?/1) do
      [] ->
        check("Faults", :ok, "Every app container is on its boot values.")

      changed ->
        check(
          "Faults",
          :warn,
          "Not on boot values: #{Enum.map_join(changed, ", ", &describe/1)}. Reset in the Control room."
        )
    end
  end

  defp boot?({_name, {:ok, %{current: same, boot: same}}}), do: true
  defp boot?(_changed), do: false

  defp describe({name, {:ok, %{current: settings}}}),
    do: "#{name} (#{Tour.Core.Faults.describe(settings)})"

  defp describe({name, {:error, _reason}}), do: "#{name} (didn't answer)"

  defp check(name, level, detail), do: %{name: name, level: level, detail: detail}

  defp first_line(text), do: text |> String.split("\n", trim: true) |> List.first("")
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.PreflightTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  alias Tour.Core.Preflight

  @down %{
    docker: {:error, "Cannot connect to the Docker daemon at unix:///var/run/docker.sock.\n"},
    repo: true,
    repo_path: "/repo",
    secret: false,
    stack: {:error, "Cannot connect to the Docker daemon"},
    prometheus: false,
    targets: {:error, "Prometheus isn't answering"},
    rules: {:error, "Prometheus isn't answering"},
    alertmanager: false,
    lookup: {:error, "The app isn't answering"},
    history: {:error, "Prometheus isn't answering"},
    faults: []
  }

  test "with everything down, every check says so in a sentence" do
    checks = Preflight.checks(@down)
    assert length(checks) == 11
    assert Enum.all?(checks, &(&1.level in [:error, :warn] or &1.name == "Checkout"))
    assert Enum.all?(checks, &(is_binary(&1.detail) and &1.detail != ""))
  end

  test "a paused load generator is a warning, and a leftover fault names its container" do
    containers =
      for {service, state} <- [
            {"app", "running"},
            {"proxy", "running"},
            {"prometheus", "running"},
            {"alertmanager", "running"},
            {"loadgen", "paused"}
          ],
          do: %{service: service, state: state}

    leftover =
      {"kev-check-app-1",
       {:ok,
        %{current: %{latency_ms: 400, error_rate: 0.2}, boot: %{latency_ms: 25, error_rate: 0.0}}}}

    checks =
      Map.new(
        Preflight.checks(%{@down | stack: {:ok, containers}, faults: [leftover]}),
        &{&1.name, &1}
      )

    assert %{level: :warn, detail: "Paused: loadgen" <> _rest} = checks["Containers"]
    assert checks["Faults"].detail =~ "kev-check-app-1 (up to 400 ms, 20% errors)"
  end
end

ExUnit.run()
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Preflight do
  @moduledoc """
  Asks Docker, Prometheus, Alertmanager, and the app how they're doing, then shows the
  verdicts as one table. Every probe fails soft, so this works with the stack down.
  """

  alias Tour.Core.Containers

  @doc "Every fact the checks need."
  @spec gather() :: map()
  def gather do
    stack = Tour.Docker.stack()

    %{
      docker: Tour.Shell.run("docker", ["version", "--format", "{{.Server.Version}}"]),
      repo: File.exists?(Path.join(Tour.Config.repo(), "compose.yaml")),
      repo_path: Tour.Config.repo(),
      secret: Tour.Config.secret?(),
      stack: stack,
      prometheus: Tour.Prom.ready?(),
      targets: Tour.Prom.targets(),
      rules: Tour.Prom.alert_rules(),
      alertmanager: Tour.Alertmanager.ready?(),
      lookup: Tour.App.lookup("CVE-2021-44228"),
      history: Tour.Prom.history_minutes(),
      faults: stack |> running_apps() |> Enum.map(&{&1.name, Tour.Docker.faults(&1.name)})
    }
  end

  @doc "The checks as a table."
  @spec board() :: Kino.Markdown.t()
  def board do
    rows =
      for check <- Tour.Core.Preflight.checks(gather()),
          do: [Tour.UI.mark(check.level), check.name, check.detail]

    Tour.UI.table(["", "Check", "What I found"], rows)
  end

  defp running_apps({:ok, containers}), do: Containers.running_apps(containers)
  defp running_apps({:error, _reason}), do: []
end
```

