## 5. Across services

The proxy returns an `x-request-id` header and each container logs it, so one request can be found across containers. Across services, the same idea is a trace.

For several services I'd use OpenTelemetry: the same request rate, error, and duration metrics and labels in every service, W3C trace context on every call, exemplars linking histogram buckets to traces, and an OpenTelemetry Collector per cluster forwarding to Chronosphere.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph cluster["One cluster"]
    A["kev-check"] -- "traceparent header" --> B["another service"]
    A -- "OTLP: metrics, traces, logs" --> C["OTel Collector"]
    B -- "OTLP" --> C
  end
  C -- "batched, sampled" --> D["Chronosphere"]
  D --> E["Dashboards and SLO alerts"]
  D -. "exemplar: slow bucket to trace" .-> F["Trace view"]
```

The latest log lines from every app container, refreshed every 5 seconds.

```elixir
columns = ["time", "container", "level", "message", "ms", "request id"]

table =
  Kino.DataTable.new([], keys: columns, name: "Latest lookups from every container", num_rows: 15)

as_row = fn row ->
  %{
    "time" => String.slice(row.time, 11, 12),
    "container" => row.pod,
    "level" => row.level,
    "message" => row.message,
    "ms" => row.duration_ms,
    "request id" => row.request_id
  }
end

rows_for = fn container ->
  case Tour.Docker.logs(container.name, 15) do
    {:ok, output} -> Tour.Core.LogLine.rows(output, container.name)
    {:error, _reason} -> []
  end
end

refresh = fn ->
  rows =
    Tour.Docker.apps()
    |> Enum.flat_map(rows_for)
    |> Enum.sort_by(& &1.time, :desc)
    |> Enum.take(30)
    |> Enum.map(as_row)

  Kino.DataTable.update(table, rows, keys: columns)
end

refresh.()
Kino.listen(5_000, fn _tick -> refresh.() end)
table
```

