## 7. Chronosphere: shaping the data

The repo's roll-up rule removes `pod` and `instance` from `http_requests_total` in `env:production`, drops the raw data, and stores at a 60 second interval. Chronosphere has four kinds of shaping rule:

| Rule                                                                                                          | When it acts                    | What's stored                                      | Saves storage? | Here                                                                     |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| [Drop](https://docs.chronosphere.io/control/shaping/rules/drop-rules)                                         | As data arrives                 | Nothing for matching series                        | Yes, all of it | `chronosphere/drop-health-checks.yaml`, in `PREVIEW`                     |
| [Roll-up](https://docs.chronosphere.io/control/shaping/rules/rollup)                                          | As data arrives, before storage | The aggregate, and the raw only without `drop_raw` | Yes            | `chronosphere/rollup-http-requests-total.yaml`, the rule this repo ships |
| [Recording](https://docs.chronosphere.io/control/shaping/rules/recording)                                     | On a schedule, after storage    | The raw data plus the query's result               | No             | `ops/prometheus/rules.yml` uses them for the SLO ratios                  |
| [Derived metric](https://docs.chronosphere.io/investigate/querying/metrics/derived-telemetry/derived-metrics) | When someone queries it         | Nothing extra, it's a saved query with a name      | No             | `chronosphere/derived-error-ratio.yaml`, the README's second query       |

`Tour.Core.RollupRule` renders the rule as chronoctl YAML and checks it against the requirements. `Tour.Core.Rollup` estimates series and point counts before and after.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Core.RollupRule do
  @moduledoc """
  Builds a Chronosphere roll-up rule from a few choices, writes it as chronoctl YAML in the
  shape of the file in `chronosphere/`, and checks it against the requirements.
  """

  @type t :: %{
          metric: String.t(),
          env: String.t(),
          discard: [String.t()],
          interval: String.t(),
          drop_raw: boolean(),
          mode: String.t()
        }

  @doc "The rule this repo needs: `http_requests_total` in production without `pod` and `instance`, raw dropped, 60s."
  @spec required() :: t()
  def required,
    do: %{
      metric: "http_requests_total",
      env: "production",
      discard: ["pod", "instance"],
      interval: "60s",
      drop_raw: true,
      mode: "ENABLED"
    }

  @doc "The rule as chronoctl YAML."
  @spec to_yaml(t()) :: String.t()
  def to_yaml(rule) do
    """
    api_version: v1/config
    kind: RollupRule
    spec:
      slug: #{String.replace(rule.metric, "_", "-")}-#{rule.env}-without-#{Enum.join(rule.discard, "-")}
      name: #{rule.metric} in #{rule.env} without #{Enum.join(rule.discard, " and ")}
      filters:
        - name: __name__
          value_glob: #{rule.metric}
        - name: env
          value_glob: #{rule.env}
      metric_name: '{{ .MetricName }}:without_#{Enum.join(rule.discard, "_")}'
      metric_type: CUMULATIVE_COUNTER
      aggregation: SUM
      interval: #{rule.interval}
      label_policy:
    #{discard(rule.discard)}
      drop_raw: #{rule.drop_raw}
      add_metric_type_label: true
      mode: #{rule.mode}
    """
  end

  @doc "Each requirement for the rule, and whether `rule` meets it."
  @spec check(t()) :: [{String.t(), boolean()}]
  def check(rule) do
    [
      {"Rolls up the counter `http_requests_total`", rule.metric == "http_requests_total"},
      {"Only in `env:production`", rule.env == "production"},
      {"Removes `pod` and `instance`", "pod" in rule.discard and "instance" in rule.discard},
      {"Removes nothing else, so `method`, `route`, and `status` survive",
       rule.discard -- ["pod", "instance"] == []},
      {"Drops the raw data", rule.drop_raw},
      {"Stores one point every 60s", rule.interval == "60s"}
    ]
  end

  @doc "True when two rule files say the same thing once comments and blank lines are gone."
  @spec same?(String.t(), String.t()) :: boolean()
  def same?(yaml, other), do: meaningful(yaml) == meaningful(other)

  defp discard([]), do: "    discard: []"
  defp discard(labels), do: "    discard:\n" <> Enum.map_join(labels, "\n", &"      - #{&1}")

  defp meaningful(text) do
    text
    |> String.split("\n")
    |> Enum.map(&String.trim_trailing/1)
    |> Enum.reject(&(&1 == "" or String.starts_with?(String.trim_leading(&1), "#")))
  end
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Core.Rollup do
  @moduledoc """
  The series math behind the roll-up: how many series the raw data has, and how many are
  left once `pod` and `instance` are gone.
  """

  @type fleet :: %{pods: pos_integer(), combos: pos_integer(), deploys_per_day: non_neg_integer()}

  @doc "Distinct series over a day, raw and rolled up. Every deploy brings a new set of pod names."
  @spec estimate(fleet()) :: %{raw_series: pos_integer(), rolled_series: pos_integer()}
  def estimate(%{pods: pods, combos: combos, deploys_per_day: deploys}),
    do: %{raw_series: pods * combos * (deploys + 1), rolled_series: combos}

  @doc "Group series by their labels once `drop` is removed, with how many merge into each."
  @spec merge([map()], [String.t()]) :: [{map(), pos_integer()}]
  def merge(series, drop) do
    series
    |> Enum.frequencies_by(&Map.drop(&1, drop))
    |> Enum.sort()
  end
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tour.ChronosphereTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  alias Tour.Core.Rollup
  alias Tour.Core.RollupRule

  test "the required rule ticks every requirement" do
    assert Enum.all?(RollupRule.check(RollupRule.required()), fn {_requirement, met?} -> met? end)
  end

  test "the builder writes the committed file, given the required values" do
    committed =
      File.read!(Path.join(Tour.Config.repo(), "chronosphere/rollup-http-requests-total.yaml"))

    assert RollupRule.same?(RollupRule.to_yaml(RollupRule.required()), committed)
  end

  test "keeping raw data or removing route fails the check" do
    checks =
      Map.new(
        RollupRule.check(%{
          RollupRule.required()
          | drop_raw: false,
            discard: ["pod", "instance", "route"]
        })
      )

    refute checks["Drops the raw data"]
    refute checks["Removes nothing else, so `method`, `route`, and `status` survive"]
    assert checks["Removes `pod` and `instance`"]
  end

  test "pods and deploys multiply raw series, and the roll-up keeps one per label set" do
    assert Rollup.estimate(%{pods: 2, combos: 3, deploys_per_day: 0}) == %{
             raw_series: 6,
             rolled_series: 3
           }

    assert Rollup.estimate(%{pods: 50, combos: 12, deploys_per_day: 10}) ==
             %{raw_series: 6_600, rolled_series: 12}
  end

  test "removing pod and instance merges one series per container into one" do
    series =
      for pod <- ["a", "b"],
          status <- ["200", "400"],
          do: %{"pod" => pod, "instance" => pod <> ":9568", "status" => status}

    assert Rollup.merge(series, ["pod", "instance"]) == [
             {%{"status" => "200"}, 2},
             {%{"status" => "400"}, 2}
           ]
  end
end

ExUnit.run()
```

Change a field to see the YAML and checklist update. The defaults match the committed file.

````elixir
builder =
  Kino.Control.form(
    [
      metric: Kino.Input.text("Metric", default: "http_requests_total"),
      env: Kino.Input.text("env", default: "production"),
      pod: Kino.Input.checkbox("Remove pod", default: true),
      instance: Kino.Input.checkbox("Remove instance", default: true),
      route: Kino.Input.checkbox("Remove route"),
      status: Kino.Input.checkbox("Remove status"),
      interval:
        Kino.Input.select("Interval", [
          {"60s", "60s"},
          {"15s", "15s"},
          {"30s", "30s"},
          {"5m", "5m"}
        ]),
      drop_raw: Kino.Input.checkbox("Drop the raw data", default: true),
      mode: Kino.Input.select("Mode", [{"ENABLED", "ENABLED"}, {"PREVIEW", "PREVIEW"}])
    ],
    report_changes: true
  )

rule_out = Tour.UI.frame()
committed_path = Path.join(Tour.Config.repo(), "chronosphere/rollup-http-requests-total.yaml")

show_rule = fn rule ->
  yaml = Tour.Core.RollupRule.to_yaml(rule)

  checklist =
    Enum.map_join(Tour.Core.RollupRule.check(rule), "\n", fn {text, met?} ->
      "* #{Tour.UI.mark(if met?, do: :ok, else: :error)} #{text}"
    end)

  file =
    case File.read(committed_path) do
      {:ok, committed} ->
        if Tour.Core.RollupRule.same?(yaml, committed),
          do:
            "#{Tour.UI.mark(:ok)} Same as `chronosphere/rollup-http-requests-total.yaml`, comments aside.",
          else: "#{Tour.UI.mark(:info)} Different from the committed file."

      {:error, _reason} ->
        "#{Tour.UI.mark(:warn)} Couldn't read the committed file."
    end

  Kino.Layout.grid(
    [
      Kino.Markdown.new("```yaml\n#{yaml}```"),
      Kino.Markdown.new("**Requirements**\n\n#{checklist}\n\n#{file}")
    ],
    columns: 2
  )
end

Kino.Frame.render(rule_out, show_rule.(Tour.Core.RollupRule.required()))

Kino.listen(builder, fn %{data: data} ->
  discard =
    for label <- [:pod, :instance, :route, :status],
        Map.fetch!(data, label),
        do: Atom.to_string(label)

  rule = %{
    metric: data.metric,
    env: data.env,
    discard: discard,
    interval: data.interval,
    drop_raw: data.drop_raw,
    mode: data.mode
  }

  Tour.UI.draw(rule_out, fn -> show_rule.(rule) end)
end)

Tour.UI.stack([builder, rule_out])
````

The rule matches `http_requests_total` series with `env=production`. Every 60 seconds it sums the increase of all series that become identical once `pod` and `instance` are removed, and stores the result as the counter `http_requests_total:without_pod_instance`. `discard` keeps every other label (`keep` would skip series missing a listed label). `drop_raw: true` means the per-pod series aren't stored.

With `drop_raw`, queries on `http_requests_total{env="production"}` return nothing once the rule is enabled, dashboards and alerts included. The rollout is `mode: PREVIEW` first, which reports impact without changing stored data, then moving dashboards and alerts to the new name, then `ENABLED`. After that, long-term storage has no per-pod data.

Each container exports its own `http_requests_total` series for every method, route, and status it has served. Without `pod` and `instance`, those merge into one series per method, route, and status. On Kubernetes each deploy renames every pod and starts a new set of series, so the raw count also grows with deploys. The roll-up also stores one point a minute instead of one per 15 second scrape.

```elixir
examples = [
  {"50 pods, 12 method, route, and status combinations",
   %{pods: 50, combos: 12, deploys_per_day: 0}},
  {"The same over a day with 10 deploys", %{pods: 50, combos: 12, deploys_per_day: 10}}
]

example_rows =
  for {label, fleet} <- examples do
    %{raw_series: raw, rolled_series: rolled} = Tour.Core.Rollup.estimate(fleet)
    [label, Tour.UI.count(raw), Tour.UI.count(rolled)]
  end

series_now = fn ->
  case Tour.Prom.query(~s|http_requests_total{env="production"}|) do
    {:ok, samples} ->
      merged = Tour.Core.Rollup.merge(Enum.map(samples, & &1.labels), ["pod", "instance"])

      now = [
        "This stack now, #{length(Tour.Docker.apps())} containers",
        length(samples),
        length(merged)
      ]

      detail =
        for {labels, n} <- merged, do: [labels["method"], labels["route"], labels["status"], n, 1]

      Tour.UI.stack([
        Tour.UI.table(["", "Raw series", "After the roll-up"], [now | example_rows]),
        Tour.UI.table(
          ["method", "route", "status", "Raw series, one per container", "After"],
          detail
        )
      ])

    {:error, reason} ->
      Tour.UI.note(:error, reason)
  end
end

recount = Kino.Control.button("Count again")
counts = Tour.UI.frame()
Tour.UI.draw(counts, series_now)
Kino.listen(recount, fn _click -> Tour.UI.draw(counts, series_now) end)
Tour.UI.stack([counts, recount])
```

